WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.568 --> 00:00:02.702
-Welcome back to "Late Night."
We're here with Isla Fisher.

00:00:02.702 --> 00:00:05.305
So, you mentioned saying
to an agent,

00:00:05.305 --> 00:00:07.140
"Can you get me
a comedy audition?"

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:09.242
But I also heard
your first agent you got

00:00:09.242 --> 00:00:12.745
when you were 11 years old,
just via the Yellow Pages.

00:00:12.745 --> 00:00:14.046
-[ Laughs ] Exactly.

00:00:14.046 --> 00:00:16.082
That's how you do it
in Perth, Australia.

00:00:16.082 --> 00:00:18.951
You go through the Yellow Pages,
you find someone.

00:00:18.951 --> 00:00:21.787
I took a bunch of photos
holding, like, a potted plant

00:00:21.787 --> 00:00:24.857
or whatever inanimate object
I could get my hands on

00:00:24.857 --> 00:00:26.992
and then proceeded
to send them to her

00:00:26.992 --> 00:00:29.295
and called her every day,
going, "I want to be a clown!

00:00:29.295 --> 00:00:31.264
Have you got me a job yet?"

00:00:31.264 --> 00:00:35.268
No, I was very enthusiastic
back then.

00:00:35.268 --> 00:00:38.838
Yeah, and that's sort of how
I started out as a kid actor.

00:00:38.838 --> 00:00:41.307
-And you were on
an Australian soap opera

00:00:41.307 --> 00:00:43.209
that a lot of incredibly
accomplished actors

00:00:43.209 --> 00:00:45.478
got their start on,
"Home and Away."

00:00:45.478 --> 00:00:47.646
But you were not resting
on your lo--

00:00:47.646 --> 00:00:49.382
laurels, excuse me,
as a young actor.

00:00:49.382 --> 00:00:53.719
You wrote books
at a very young age.

00:00:53.719 --> 00:00:56.723
-Were you going to say, "You
were not resting on your loins"?

00:00:56.723 --> 00:00:59.292
[ Laughing ] I was like --
-I was gonna say "loins."

00:00:59.292 --> 00:01:01.761
I -- Look, I have a bad back,

00:01:01.761 --> 00:01:04.497
and my doctor said I have to
rest on my loins more often.

00:01:04.497 --> 00:01:06.966
-[ Laughs ]

00:01:06.966 --> 00:01:08.634
Okay, I --
Yes, you're right.

00:01:08.634 --> 00:01:10.936
I wrote romance novels
with my mother -- two of them,

00:01:10.936 --> 00:01:13.272
"Seduced by Fame" and
"Bewitched" -- when I was 18,

00:01:13.272 --> 00:01:14.940
which was actually
very embarrassing

00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:18.377
'cause I was a pretty
inexperienced teenager.

00:01:18.377 --> 00:01:20.847
Not surprising,
given my penchant for clowns.

00:01:20.847 --> 00:01:23.783
So, when she would -- we had to
write these romance scenes,

00:01:23.783 --> 00:01:27.654
which were quite -- well, let's
just say they got hot and heavy.

00:01:27.654 --> 00:01:29.722
I had no idea
what Mum was pitching.

00:01:29.722 --> 00:01:31.490
Some of the things she said,
I was so --

00:01:31.490 --> 00:01:33.526
I just didn't --
It was so awkward.

00:01:33.526 --> 00:01:34.661
Anyway.

00:01:34.661 --> 00:01:36.462
Now, of course,
I've actually moved into

00:01:36.462 --> 00:01:38.264
sort of short-form kids books,

00:01:38.264 --> 00:01:41.533
and I've written four
"Marge in Charge," a series,

00:01:41.533 --> 00:01:43.837
and I'm just developing a show
now with Nickelodeon.

00:01:43.837 --> 00:01:46.339
So, I still --
I still love to write,

00:01:46.339 --> 00:01:50.176
but thankfully not sex scenes,
and not with my mum.

00:01:50.176 --> 00:01:52.745
-So, you do have children now,
and I'm wondering,

00:01:52.745 --> 00:01:55.081
are they of the age where
they read "Marge in Charge"?

00:01:55.081 --> 00:01:57.316
And if so, do they like them?

00:01:57.316 --> 00:02:01.220
-Does -- I, mean I feel
like I started --

00:02:01.220 --> 00:02:06.059
I started them, oh, gosh,
at least six years ago now.

00:02:06.059 --> 00:02:09.629
And so a lot of the voice of
Marge came out of, you know --

00:02:09.629 --> 00:02:11.497
You know how you do impressions
for your kids at bedtime?

00:02:11.497 --> 00:02:13.365
-Yes.
-You know, Peppa Pig

00:02:13.365 --> 00:02:16.468
or, like, their friends' parents
or whatever to get a laugh.

00:02:16.468 --> 00:02:18.171
Marge sort of began as a voice,

00:02:18.171 --> 00:02:21.274
and then, yes,
she turned into these books.

00:02:21.274 --> 00:02:23.409
And so they were
and they are a --

00:02:23.409 --> 00:02:25.077
continue to be a hit
in the house.

00:02:25.077 --> 00:02:26.345
So, I'm very grateful.

00:02:26.345 --> 00:02:28.113
But equally,
they're very good critics,

00:02:28.113 --> 00:02:30.349
and I get told
when something isn't funny

00:02:30.349 --> 00:02:32.451
or some character
can't do something

00:02:32.451 --> 00:02:34.920
when I read the stories
before I hand them in.

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:36.522
They're the real editors.

00:02:36.522 --> 00:02:38.858
[ Laughs ]
Yeah.

00:02:38.858 --> 00:02:41.928
-It's nice both that they have
the comfort to tell you that,

00:02:41.928 --> 00:02:44.997
and I think it's equally nice
that you trust their opinion.

00:02:44.997 --> 00:02:47.132
I think that speaks
to a very strong

00:02:47.132 --> 00:02:48.768
parent-child relationship.

00:02:48.768 --> 00:02:51.203
-Aw, thanks, Seth.

00:02:51.203 --> 00:02:54.741
-I'm also very -- I have a lot
of respect for your family

00:02:54.741 --> 00:02:57.376
because you have the same kind
of dog that I grew up with.

00:02:57.376 --> 00:02:58.678
You have a sheepdog.

00:02:58.678 --> 00:03:00.079
Is it Maisy?
Is that the name?

00:03:00.079 --> 00:03:02.214
-Maisy, yes!
Yeah!

00:03:02.214 --> 00:03:03.682
[ Laughs ]
Whoa, whoa, whoa.

00:03:03.682 --> 00:03:06.752
-So, this is -- this picture
put me in the best mood

00:03:06.752 --> 00:03:08.154
because, you know, my parents

00:03:08.154 --> 00:03:09.555
still do this
with their sheepdog.

00:03:09.555 --> 00:03:11.424
It is --
I think people are shocked

00:03:11.424 --> 00:03:14.326
when they see exactly
how little these dogs are

00:03:14.326 --> 00:03:15.562
underneath all that hair.

00:03:15.562 --> 00:03:17.463
-Right!
They're just like "Who are you?"

00:03:17.463 --> 00:03:21.467
Underneath the burly fur
is a wafer-thin dog.

00:03:21.467 --> 00:03:24.737
But truthfully, I feel like
she'd be really insulted

00:03:24.737 --> 00:03:27.139
if she knew
that I shared that picture.

00:03:27.139 --> 00:03:29.475
I feel like I have to
run everything by her

00:03:29.475 --> 00:03:31.977
before I post it on Instagram,
because she --

00:03:31.977 --> 00:03:35.614
You can see she just
feels so self-conscious.

00:03:35.614 --> 00:03:37.550
But we had to shave her,
unfortunately,

00:03:37.550 --> 00:03:40.853
because she got dreadlocks.

00:03:40.853 --> 00:03:43.990
And anyway, you know what it's
like with an English sheepdog.

00:03:43.990 --> 00:03:46.959
It's grooming daily.
-Yeah.

00:03:46.959 --> 00:03:49.795
I feel like half of the time,

00:03:49.795 --> 00:03:53.932
my mother was just
straddling the dog,

00:03:53.932 --> 00:03:56.469
like, just working out --

00:03:56.469 --> 00:03:58.737
Like, my memories of my mom
growing up were, like,

00:03:58.737 --> 00:04:02.508
just her, like, just fighting
this losing battle with --

00:04:02.508 --> 00:04:05.611
-I know, that's what --
It's just brushing.

00:04:05.611 --> 00:04:07.547
Like, I look in the mirror
and I think,

00:04:07.547 --> 00:04:10.616
"I haven't brushed my hair,
but Maisy's fur is beautiful."

00:04:10.616 --> 00:04:12.351
[ Both laugh ]

00:04:12.351 --> 00:04:14.387
-Now, "Wolf Like Me."
This is a --

00:04:14.387 --> 00:04:17.089
Is it right that this was
a friend of yours

00:04:17.089 --> 00:04:19.391
who had this idea, someone
you've known for a long time?

00:04:19.391 --> 00:04:22.861
-Yeah, Bruna Papandrea.
She's a fantastic producer.

00:04:22.861 --> 00:04:25.464
She did "Big Little Lies"
and "Nine Perfect Strangers."

00:04:25.464 --> 00:04:28.568
And she came to me with this --

00:04:28.568 --> 00:04:29.969
you know, with this idea
that was --

00:04:29.969 --> 00:04:32.338
it was very, you know, specific.

00:04:32.338 --> 00:04:34.841
Again, I can't really
give it away.

00:04:34.841 --> 00:04:36.041
But I just connected to it.

00:04:36.041 --> 00:04:37.810
And I really love
the director's --

00:04:37.810 --> 00:04:40.579
you know, his film
and just his take on it.

00:04:40.579 --> 00:04:42.281
And it just felt like --

00:04:42.281 --> 00:04:44.250
you know, it just felt
like the right step.

00:04:44.250 --> 00:04:48.387
You know, I prefer, honestly, to
work in comedy, but this was --

00:04:48.387 --> 00:04:51.390
for a dramatic role,
it was very complex,

00:04:51.390 --> 00:04:55.428
and she's a great character,
you know, to portray.

00:04:55.428 --> 00:04:58.463
-You mentioned it early on
when we were talking.

00:04:58.463 --> 00:05:01.467
We did meet on the set of
"Hot Rod," which is a film --

00:05:01.467 --> 00:05:04.203
-Yeah, you are
the greatest writer.

00:05:04.203 --> 00:05:06.071
-Oh, that's very kind of you.

00:05:06.071 --> 00:05:08.441
-You -- No, I'm just
gonna say this now.

00:05:08.441 --> 00:05:10.542
I had never had
a creative experience

00:05:10.542 --> 00:05:12.611
and haven't really since

00:05:12.611 --> 00:05:14.847
like the one where
I sat in a room with you

00:05:14.847 --> 00:05:16.081
and you were just --

00:05:16.081 --> 00:05:17.984
'cause at that point
with the character Denise,

00:05:17.984 --> 00:05:19.318
we didn't know
if she was gonna be comedic

00:05:19.318 --> 00:05:21.019
or whether she was gonna
be more the straight man,

00:05:21.019 --> 00:05:23.322
'cause Rod was, you know,
a heightened character.

00:05:23.322 --> 00:05:24.824
Anyway, and you
were just like --

00:05:24.824 --> 00:05:27.459
you could write a joke
for any type of character,

00:05:27.459 --> 00:05:28.694
for any scene.

00:05:28.694 --> 00:05:31.363
You know, and they were
character-based humor,

00:05:31.363 --> 00:05:35.368
but there was also just, like,
puns and just sight gags.

00:05:35.368 --> 00:05:38.237
And I just feel like
you are so brilliant.

00:05:38.237 --> 00:05:40.139
Anyway.
-That was not --

00:05:40.139 --> 00:05:42.007
That was not
what I was fishing for,

00:05:42.007 --> 00:05:43.442
but it turned out great for me.

00:05:43.442 --> 00:05:45.244
This is --
I'm just gonna say,

00:05:45.244 --> 00:05:48.280
this is the best Raya date
I've ever been on.

00:05:48.280 --> 00:05:51.050
I should've dressed up for it.

00:05:51.050 --> 00:05:53.820
But I will say, you know,
I think we can both admit,

00:05:53.820 --> 00:05:56.422
"Hot Rod" did not take
the box office by storm.

00:05:56.422 --> 00:05:58.591
It has really grown
into a cult classic,

00:05:58.591 --> 00:06:01.627
and I hope you hear as many
nice things about it as I do.

00:06:01.627 --> 00:06:03.229
-Continually.
-Yeah.

00:06:03.229 --> 00:06:05.465
-Yeah, more than anything
I've ever done,

00:06:05.465 --> 00:06:07.967
I get asked about "Hot Rod."

00:06:07.967 --> 00:06:10.602
And people --
when Halloween comes around

00:06:10.602 --> 00:06:11.870
and every time I go to a party,

00:06:11.870 --> 00:06:14.172
there's Denise and Rod
or just Rod.

00:06:14.172 --> 00:06:17.009
I mean, I feel like it really
got into the zeitgeist

00:06:17.009 --> 00:06:19.144
and became --
Is it a classic?

00:06:19.144 --> 00:06:20.746
I don't know if that's
too strong a word,

00:06:20.746 --> 00:06:22.414
but it's definitely --

00:06:22.414 --> 00:06:26.218
Everybody seems to use it
as their comfort comedy film

00:06:26.218 --> 00:06:28.220
to cheer themselves up
again and again.

00:06:28.220 --> 00:06:29.889
-Well, it's very --
it's very special

00:06:29.889 --> 00:06:32.324
to have had even a small part
in something like that.

00:06:32.324 --> 00:06:34.360
And it's really great
to see you again, Isla.

00:06:34.360 --> 00:06:36.696
Thank you so much
for making time for us today.

00:06:36.696 --> 00:06:39.031
-Thank you, Seth.
Thank you so much.

00:06:39.031 --> 00:06:41.133
Take care, mate.
-Isla Fisher, everybody.

00:06:41.133 --> 00:06:43.403
"Wolf Like Me" begins streaming
on Peacock tomorrow.

